WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.599 --> 00:00:07.950
- Well, good afternoon everyone.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:08.790
We're really pleased

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:13.790
to have next one of our deans
invited lecture series here.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:20.940
Today we have Bratin Saha from Amazon AWS.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:24.990
This series really is
intended to emphasize

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.480
the opportunities for connections

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.680
between the School of Engineering
and Yale and industry.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.630
And our conversations today with Bratin

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.000
have really driven home that point.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.730
We've been jumping from topic to topic,

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:40.110
all kinds of different ways,

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.260
that what's going on at
the university interrelates

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.300
and can impact what's going on at Amazon

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.550
and certainly vice versa.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.020
Part of the infrastructure

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.510
that Bratin has been responsible
for developing at Amazon

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.220
is really the backbone

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:57.360
that will power the transformative tools

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.430
that we're witnessing
in industry right now

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:01.530
with generative AI.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:05.100
This is a massively
computationally rich area,

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.710
but also a massively
computationally expensive

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:09.840
and data driven area.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.620
So what I hope we'll learn today

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.520
is sort of ways that he
sees a future interaction

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.620
between Yale and Amazon,

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.380
being productive and
profitable for all of us.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.230
So with that, I would love to welcome.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.630
So I will say, Bratin was I think,

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:29.970
Zhong Shao's first PhD student.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:30.803
Is that correct?

00:01:30.803 --> 00:01:33.390
Yeah, so Zhong was the
chair of computer science,

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:36.670
certainly, the whole time
I've been here until this year

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:41.220
and it's really wonderful to see

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.040
these birds that have taken flight,

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:45.933
come back home to roost

00:01:45.933 --> 00:01:46.949
(both laughing)

00:01:46.949 --> 00:01:48.690
and share their experiences with us.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.480
So he's going to give a introduction

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:53.370
and tell us a little
bit about his trajectory

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.470
and then he and I will have a brief chat

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:58.373
and then you guys can
ask him some questions.

00:01:58.373 --> 00:01:59.700
Okay. So with that, take it away.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.554
Thank you, Dean Brock. Thank you.

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:05.280
(audience applauding)

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.757
- It's nice to be back home.

00:02:06.757 --> 00:02:08.760
You know, I spent a lot of time,

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.270
a lot of days and nights at
the A.K. Watson in the zoo

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:13.170
and other places

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:17.670
and it was really nice to see
how the university has evolved

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.100
over the last many years,
thanks to leadership

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:24.120
from Dean Brock, the Yale
leadership, John himself,

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:25.890
and seeing the amount of investments

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.830
that are being made in computer
science and engineering.

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:32.310
So what I want to talk
about today is some ways

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:35.220
in which we have been using generative AI

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.740
and enabling customers in the enterprise

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.060
to use generative AI.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.490
Today, most of the machine learning,

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.170
vast majority of machine learning

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:47.010
and AI in the world happens on AWS,

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:50.700
happens on the infrastructure
that my team builds.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.560
And what I wanted to talk about

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:56.460
and where I'm hoping you
can get some insights on

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:58.690
is where the industry is going

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:02.160
and what would be the research problems

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:03.510
that would be interesting,

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:06.000
especially from an industry perspective,

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.400
where the science sees,

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.890
in terms of where we can
take it out to customers

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.930
and where the science isn't in

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:15.600
terms of where I think there's
a lot of fundamental research

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.523
to be done in a university like Yale.

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:19.803
So let me get started.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:27.090
So, there really have been
a set of tipping points

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:29.395
around generative AI.

00:03:29.395 --> 00:03:33.030
One of them has been just
the astonishing increase

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:35.220
in the amount of compute capability.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:38.910
If you think about IT, if you
think about computer science,

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:40.830
it was driven by Moore's law.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.630
And Moore's law said

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:45.300
that the amount of
compute is going to double

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.220
every 18 months.

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.130
What we have seen in
AI and machine learning

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:52.200
for the last 10 years

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.140
is that compute capacity has been doubling

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:57.303
every three and a half months.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:01.785
We are going at six
times, literally six times

00:04:01.785 --> 00:04:03.930
the speed of Moore's law.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.030
We are going literally
at six times the speed

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:07.680
at which IT used to go.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.440
And IT evolved really, really fast.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:13.050
Even if you look at the last five years,

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:15.300
that's about 30 years
of progress in IT land.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:19.380
And now you go back 30
years, there was no Amazon,

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:22.320
no Google, no Facebook, no
Meta, no Twitter and so on.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.680
So this field is moving extremely fast.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.690
The other thing that has happened,

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:31.290
is the emergence of internet scale data,

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:33.000
and I'll give you a little bit of insight

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.080
into what that is doing
with large language models.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.030
And then the third thing that has happened

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:40.980
is the amount of machine
learning innovation.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:43.920
And on the next slide I'm going
to talk to you about this.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:45.720
But the other thing that has also happened

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:49.470
that I think is really interesting
for Yale as a university,

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:52.830
for students and faculty here
is the role of the cloud.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.920
Because what we have done
is we have made it possible

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:59.670
to make this compute and
this data readily available

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:03.900
to anybody that wants to use
it at a very, very cheap cost.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:06.723
So what has this meant for innovation?

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:10.713
If you think about machine learning,

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:14.910
the capacity of machine learning models,

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:18.150
and you can measure it
by a number of parameters

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:22.463
just in the last three
years has grown by 1600x.

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:27.213
This is super, super, super exponential.

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.440
And that is what is driving
amazing innovations.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:36.420
Now what does that mean
from a data perspective?

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:41.420
If you think about we humans,
in the course of a lifetime,

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:44.466
an average human, we'll probably listen

00:05:44.466 --> 00:05:47.793
to about a billion words
or 2 billion words.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:51.480
When we train these foundation models,

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:54.423
we train them on trillions of words.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:58.543
So each one of these foundation
models is being trained

00:05:58.543 --> 00:06:01.748
thousands of times of data

00:06:01.748 --> 00:06:06.003
that an average human will
be consuming in the lifetime.

00:06:07.682 --> 00:06:11.340
If I give you another perspective on this,

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:16.340
all of Wikipedia is about 18
gigabytes, 18 to 20 gigabytes.

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:18.540
When we train these models,

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:22.773
we train them on trillions
of terabytes of data.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:27.060
And that means that you're
putting in the knowledge

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:31.470
of thousands of Wikipedias
into one of these models.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:35.910
And that is what is triggering
this amazing innovation

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:38.250
that is coming on in generative AI.

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:42.300
Now, there's a lot of research to be done,

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.850
in terms of making it really deployable

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:46.320
at scale in the industry

00:06:46.320 --> 00:06:49.833
and some of that is what
I'm going to talk about.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.880
So this innovation can
transform industries,

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.680
it will transform industries.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.420
And I'll give you a little bit of sense

00:06:57.420 --> 00:06:59.763
of some of the places
where we are doing this.

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:05.040
So let me start with Amazon CodeWhisperer.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:08.820
This is a software development tool

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:11.188
that is based on generative AI.

00:07:11.188 --> 00:07:16.188
We have seen a lot of companies
now start using this tool.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:19.860
So let me actually show
you how this works.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:24.860
So all you are doing is, as a programmer,

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:27.120
you are just writing comments,

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.640
you're just putting in
English language text.

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:32.767
So the programmer came in and said,

00:07:32.767 --> 00:07:35.220
"Just a write me a program

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:39.837
to upload a file from S3,
using server side encryption."

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:45.800
Then CodeWhisperer
actually takes this command

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:50.100
and then CodeWhisperer
is just going to come in

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:53.613
and generate this file by itself.

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:56.790
This is all automatically generated.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.380
As a programmer, you just have
to write this English stuff

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:09.510
and then CodeWhisperer will
give you a few hints about it

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:14.340
and then you come in and
this is another Java thing,

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:18.210
and all you're doing
is, you're writing this,

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:21.090
it went too fast, it
said, "Create some stuff,"

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:23.850
And CodeWhisperer is
going to basically create

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:25.353
the entire function for you.

00:08:26.729 --> 00:08:28.593
And what we have seen,

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:33.637
so this is what that programmer writes,

00:08:33.637 --> 00:08:35.517
"Create a function to do this."

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:39.070
And it'll generate the
entire function for you.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:44.550
And then you can go in

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:46.710
and run some checks
and so on and so forth.

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:48.360
So what does that mean?

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:53.360
What does this mean for the
software development industry

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:58.860
and what does it mean for
a university like Yale?

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:07.500
We have studies that show people

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:10.090
that are using CodeWhisperer-like tools

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:14.493
are about 50% more productive
than people who don't,

00:09:15.433 --> 00:09:17.003
because you're not doing your own coding.

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:20.370
But there's something
more profound going on,

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:21.203
and these tools, by the way,

00:09:21.203 --> 00:09:23.910
are going to keep getting
better every year.

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:25.860
So imagine five years from now,

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:27.603
when these tools are really good.

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:31.350
What is going on is
something very profound here.

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:36.210
It's that Java and Python and Rust

00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:39.180
are no longer the programming languages.

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:41.673
English is the new programming language.

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:46.230
And you can imagine that we
are going to be able to do this

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:49.143
with say, Italian or Chinese
or Hindi or something.

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:52.150
And so our mother tongues

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:55.533
become the new programming language.

00:09:56.850 --> 00:09:58.860
What does this mean for Yale?

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:03.860
How should computer science
teaching evolve to address this?

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:07.530
And I was talking in fact this morning

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:09.660
with Dean Brock about how do you bring

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:12.450
more liberal arts education to STEM,

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:14.190
to computer science and engineering?

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:15.720
But this may basically mean

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:17.670
that anyone who is an English major

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:19.020
or history major, whatever,

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:21.990
is now going to become
a software developer,

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:24.300
five years, ten years from now.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.333
And so when you have
democratized this to some extent,

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:31.080
what does it mean for
innovation in the world

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:34.113
when every person is a software developer?

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:39.390
And these are I think things
that a university like Yale

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:43.200
should think about, because
it's really kind of a university

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:45.060
that's at the forefront of,

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:47.310
how does technology impact society

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:50.760
five to ten years from now
and how do we educate people

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:51.593
in that way?

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:56.610
The next one I'll talk about

00:10:56.610 --> 00:10:59.220
is what's happening in medicine.

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:01.980
So there is a lot of work
we are doing in my team

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:06.510
and elsewhere in terms of
applying AI to medicine.

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:08.760
And the reason that is
happening as you can see,

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:10.533
just like with Moore's Law,

00:11:14.431 --> 00:11:15.750
the cost of sequencing a
human genome is dropping

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:19.980
in a very fundamental way.

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:22.983
It used to be a huge amount,

00:11:24.212 --> 00:11:27.270
few million, hundreds of
millions, 20 years back,

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:29.523
to now it's about $200.

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:34.050
And because this cost has
dropped so dramatically,

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:38.250
you can now spurn an entire
industry that's based on this.

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:42.900
In fact, so we have these challenges,

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:45.660
in terms of being able
to do genomic sequencing

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:47.793
and as a result of that,

00:11:50.368 --> 00:11:53.781
I was actually talking
to someone yesterday,

00:11:53.781 --> 00:11:57.720
yesterday at dinner, who
is at Columbia University,

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:00.450
and they were actually talking
about, "Hey, we also want,"

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:02.580
and I'm sure the same thing is true here

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:04.530
in the school of medicine

00:12:04.530 --> 00:12:07.230
or even in the engineering departments

00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:10.380
that are trying to do some AI in medicine,

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:12.300
that you have a lot of
infrastructure issues

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:13.470
you have to deal with,

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:17.073
if you want to do that
kind of genomic sequencing.

00:12:18.182 --> 00:12:22.510
That is why we came out with this service

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:28.050
that is actually being used
by a lot of universities

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:30.210
and public health departments now,

00:12:30.210 --> 00:12:35.010
to be able to do gene sequencing
and precision medicine.

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:40.010
And in fact, the Department
of Public Health in Chicago,

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:44.100
they were able to use these
tools to run some analysis

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:46.200
and they found that when
you go like 10 miles

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:49.230
from downtown Chicago,
your life expectancy falls

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:51.210
by like eight or ten or twelve years.

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:52.470
And the reason that happens

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:54.960
is because of difference in access

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:57.720
to specific kinds of care.

00:12:57.720 --> 00:12:59.100
And they were able to do that,

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:01.110
because they were able to use these tools

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:04.590
to see what kind of medical
care isn't being afforded there

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:06.270
and they have now changed

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:08.760
the way that medical care is being given.

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:10.830
You know, similarly, Genomics England,

00:13:10.830 --> 00:13:15.830
they have a huge database of,
probably the world's largest,

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:19.225
of humans who have genetic mutations

00:13:19.225 --> 00:13:21.120
with some rare diseases,

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:22.920
and they're now using these tools

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:24.510
to do a lot of precision medicine.

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:28.920
And I do think that medicine is a domain

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:32.100
that is ripe for a lot of innovation,

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:35.913
from computer science, from
AI and a lot of other fields.

00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:38.880
I'll give you another example

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:41.310
of the kind of innovation that we can do.

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:45.930
So patients today are
walking with tons of data.

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:48.393
You have all of your wearables

00:13:49.797 --> 00:13:51.870
and you're getting all kinds of data

00:13:51.870 --> 00:13:56.870
from multiple different data sources.

00:13:57.930 --> 00:14:02.100
But the thing is, 97% of this
data is not being analyzed,

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:03.123
it's not being used.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:08.040
And so imagine the quality
of patient outcomes

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:09.330
that we could improve

00:14:09.330 --> 00:14:13.023
if there's a way for us to
be able to analyze this data.

00:14:14.430 --> 00:14:17.220
And this, being able to analyze this data,

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:21.360
I think is another really
interesting research

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.340
that I think a university like Yale

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:24.780
would be perfectly suited for.

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:27.537
Because you have a school
of medicine, that's a leader

00:14:27.537 --> 00:14:29.790
and you have the engineering
parts, that's a leader,

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:31.830
you have the data science
parts, that's a leader.

00:14:31.830 --> 00:14:34.110
And so this kind of interdisciplinary work

00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:35.790
would just be amazing,

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:38.370
in terms of improving patient outcomes.

00:14:38.370 --> 00:14:41.430
Now, there's one way in
which we are doing this,

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:44.880
which is clinical documentation.

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:47.403
So today, when you go to a physician,

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:51.000
because of electronic health records,

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:53.970
that interaction that you're
having with the physician

00:14:53.970 --> 00:14:55.830
needs to be scribed.

00:14:55.830 --> 00:14:58.560
And so the physicians
are actually doing it

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.000
and then creating a summary

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.200
and then they're uploading it

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:03.420
to your electronic health records.

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:07.800
It turns out physicians
in the US, on average,

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:12.800
spend about 40% of the
time doing the scribing.

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:15.690
And that is the time
that is not being spent

00:15:15.690 --> 00:15:16.530
on patient care,

00:15:16.530 --> 00:15:19.490
that's the time that is
really an unproductive use

00:15:19.490 --> 00:15:20.853
of the physician's time.

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:22.920
And so what we have done,

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:25.980
and we have launched this
product a little bit,

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:29.280
is using generative AI

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:33.687
to automatically generate these
patient physician summaries.

00:15:34.770 --> 00:15:37.590
So as you're talking to a physician,

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:40.710
that particular conversation
is getting captured,

00:15:40.710 --> 00:15:42.810
it's automatically getting transcribed.

00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:45.030
A generative AI model is automatically

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:47.100
generating the summary.

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:49.740
Now, the key innovation that we did here,

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:52.380
that I think is very relevant also

00:15:52.380 --> 00:15:54.120
from a research point of view,

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:57.840
is as you know, generative AI
can also have hallucinations.

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:00.930
So you need to have a very
high degree of accuracy.

00:16:00.930 --> 00:16:02.640
And so one of the things that we did here,

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:06.030
which I think was a pretty
nice piece of innovation,

00:16:06.030 --> 00:16:08.160
but there's a lot more
research to be done,

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:10.350
is what's called the concept of grounding.

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:14.100
So we have the original
transcript, we create a summary,

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:15.780
but every part of that summary,

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:18.757
we put a link to the
original source that says,

00:16:18.757 --> 00:16:21.180
"Here is why I created the summary."

00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:26.180
And that is what helps make
this deployable to enterprises.

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:32.270
This is supposed to reduce
the scribing time by 70%.

00:16:35.970 --> 00:16:37.920
We are partnering with 3M,

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:40.980
who have 300,000 physicians across the US.

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:44.190
Now imagine reducing the time

00:16:44.190 --> 00:16:46.620
of making them more productive by 70%

00:16:46.620 --> 00:16:49.200
for 300,000 physicians in the US.

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:51.390
In fact, I was looking
at all of the places

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:53.427
that this will go into POC,

00:16:53.427 --> 00:16:54.900
and I was really glad to know

00:16:54.900 --> 00:16:57.550
that Yale New Haven Hospital
is actually one of them.

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:02.010
And the reason I go into this example,

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:05.190
is to say that you may be looking
at a lot of generative AI,

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:07.350
some of it may seem to be hype,

00:17:07.350 --> 00:17:11.670
but a lot of it is actually
being put into real production

00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:13.470
at scale deployment.

00:17:13.470 --> 00:17:15.630
And so all of the
research that you can do,

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:17.820
in terms of making it accurate,

00:17:17.820 --> 00:17:20.130
in terms of reducing hallucinations,

00:17:20.130 --> 00:17:22.500
in terms of finding ways
to do the grounding,

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:25.263
actually helps in these situations.

00:17:29.190 --> 00:17:31.470
So here is how it actually works.

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:32.790
I already talked about this.

00:17:32.790 --> 00:17:36.990
So the patient says,
this one is not showing,

00:17:36.990 --> 00:17:38.520
you can see the highlighted parts,

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:40.860
the patient says, "This
is what is going on."

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:44.468
And we take the whole summary
and then we create it.

00:17:44.468 --> 00:17:45.390
And you're not seeing it here,

00:17:45.390 --> 00:17:47.130
but we'll actually have links,

00:17:47.130 --> 00:17:49.650
we'll actually have references from here,

00:17:49.650 --> 00:17:51.568
that says, this part came from here,

00:17:51.568 --> 00:17:53.883
this part came from here.

00:17:55.920 --> 00:17:58.503
And all of this is being
automatically generated.

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:08.810
So what do you need to do
generative AI in the enterprise?

00:18:10.347 --> 00:18:14.460
The first one is you really
want to have an easy way

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:17.725
of building with foundation models,

00:18:17.725 --> 00:18:19.020
and you want to have a choice of building

00:18:19.020 --> 00:18:20.100
with foundation models,

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:23.400
and the second is then infrastructure.

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:24.720
And that in both of these,

00:18:24.720 --> 00:18:26.190
there's a lot of research to be done,

00:18:26.190 --> 00:18:27.360
especially in the second one

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:29.340
and I'll talk a little bit on that.

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:34.340
So the first one was the kind
of models that you can use.

00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:37.260
Because you use Bedrock,

00:18:37.260 --> 00:18:39.210
there's a bunch of other stuff to use,

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:41.220
and I think the key thing

00:18:41.220 --> 00:18:45.420
that comes out from a research perspective

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:48.090
is that it's very likely

00:18:48.090 --> 00:18:51.240
that one model isn't
going to rule them all.

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:54.690
And so there is a need for figuring out

00:18:54.690 --> 00:18:58.200
what kinds of models work
well in what situations.

00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:01.980
And the thing that I think
will really distinguish

00:19:01.980 --> 00:19:05.010
going forward is not just
the models but the data

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:07.200
that is being used to train them.

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:09.660
And in fact, that's where Dean Brock,

00:19:09.660 --> 00:19:12.900
which is, can Yale play a role,

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:17.430
given the amount of data you
have across the university,

00:19:17.430 --> 00:19:20.550
not just in the engineering
and in the social sciences,

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:23.040
in all of the libraries and all of this,

00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:25.440
is there a role that Yale can play

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:29.490
in making that data
available as training data?

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:30.840
Because that would be huge

00:19:30.840 --> 00:19:32.950
and that would not just be huge

00:19:32.950 --> 00:19:36.720
from a research perspective
in moving frontiers,

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:38.047
but I think

00:19:38.047 --> 00:19:39.840
that would be a huge source
of monetization as well,

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:41.880
if Yale could figure out how to do that.

00:19:41.880 --> 00:19:46.227
So the first thing in
here is the set of models

00:19:46.227 --> 00:19:49.800
and we have some of the
most state of the art models

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:51.003
that you can play with.

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:55.050
And then there are a lot more models

00:19:55.050 --> 00:19:57.540
that are available on SageMaker Jumpstart.

00:19:57.540 --> 00:19:59.880
Now foundation models

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:03.554
can do a lot of natural
language interaction with you,

00:20:03.554 --> 00:20:08.554
but foundation models
cannot execute tasks yet

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:09.803
on your behalf.

00:20:09.803 --> 00:20:13.387
So for example, let's say you said,

00:20:13.387 --> 00:20:16.830
"Give me all of the
options for having a plane

00:20:16.830 --> 00:20:20.157
fly to Florida over Thanksgiving."

00:20:21.240 --> 00:20:22.920
Today they're probably going to give you

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:24.997
all of those options,
but then you would say,

00:20:24.997 --> 00:20:27.150
"And by the way, go make
the bookings for me."

00:20:27.150 --> 00:20:30.000
So that action part is still not there.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:33.660
And that is why we
actually augmented Bedrock

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:35.160
with what we call agents,

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:38.040
where agents can take
actions on your behalf.

00:20:38.040 --> 00:20:40.920
Now, there's a lot of research going on,

00:20:40.920 --> 00:20:42.390
how do you make it safe?

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:44.820
How do you make it secure,

00:20:44.820 --> 00:20:49.567
so it works in a particular
constrained set of environments,

00:20:50.820 --> 00:20:52.920
constrained set of actions.

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:55.260
And so there's a lot of
research to be done here,

00:20:55.260 --> 00:20:57.780
but you can clearly see that
is where the world is headed.

00:20:57.780 --> 00:21:00.780
You can clearly see that
you're going to be having

00:21:00.780 --> 00:21:02.610
some kind of a personal assistant

00:21:02.610 --> 00:21:06.540
and say, "Hey, I need these to be bought.

00:21:06.540 --> 00:21:08.670
Go get this from the web."

00:21:08.670 --> 00:21:12.720
And so this notion of having
actions and assistance,

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:13.800
all of this is coming.

00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:16.380
And so there's a lot of
really interesting research

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:19.230
to be done, in terms of
how do you secure it?

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:21.540
How do you ensure privacy of the data?

00:21:21.540 --> 00:21:23.729
How do you ensure that
the actions being taken

00:21:23.729 --> 00:21:26.950
are well-meaning? And so on and so forth

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:30.900
Okay, next,

00:21:30.900 --> 00:21:35.160
let me start a little bit
around the infrastructure.

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:37.780
Most of this is on GPUs

00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:42.450
and of course we have our
own custom silicon as well.

00:21:42.450 --> 00:21:45.457
That's purpose built for generative AI

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:55.200
and there's just a ton of work to be done.

00:21:55.200 --> 00:21:58.170
There's a ton of systems
research to be done

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:00.513
in making this more efficient.

00:22:02.624 --> 00:22:06.360
Even now there's a lot
of investment to be done

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:07.680
in training these models.

00:22:07.680 --> 00:22:09.840
And at every layer, at the hardware layer,

00:22:09.840 --> 00:22:12.660
at the system software
layer, at the tooling layer,

00:22:12.660 --> 00:22:14.557
there's a ton of innovation to be done

00:22:14.557 --> 00:22:16.500
in terms of figuring out

00:22:16.500 --> 00:22:18.300
how we make this a lot more efficient.

00:22:18.300 --> 00:22:20.160
And we have some ideas,

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:23.820
but if people are looking for
interesting research ideas,

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:27.330
this is where there's a
lot of work to be done.

00:22:27.330 --> 00:22:29.553
And then there is tooling.

00:22:31.110 --> 00:22:34.320
What happens, and again, there's
a lot of HPC related stuff

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:35.640
that you can do here,

00:22:35.640 --> 00:22:40.080
the thing that's different
about generative AI especially

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:41.910
is the scale of compute.

00:22:41.910 --> 00:22:43.380
And so, as you're training,

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:46.020
let's say these foundation models,

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:49.710
you're probably training
them on thousands of GPUs

00:22:49.710 --> 00:22:53.640
or other accelerators,
maybe for months at end.

00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:56.580
And what then used to happen before was,

00:22:56.580 --> 00:22:58.980
what about the resiliency
of the accelerator?

00:22:58.980 --> 00:23:00.330
What if one of the accelerators

00:23:00.330 --> 00:23:01.980
for some reason has an error?

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:03.120
How do you recover from it?

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:06.090
Because you don't want
to waste months of work.

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:09.150
And so, that is where things
like self-healing systems

00:23:09.150 --> 00:23:09.983
that we have,

00:23:09.983 --> 00:23:12.150
but you can obviously do more there

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:15.390
and other kinds of optimizations,
become really important.

00:23:15.390 --> 00:23:20.190
And so for those of you working
the system software side,

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:24.343
this is really fertile territory
for the kind of innovations

00:23:24.343 --> 00:23:28.203
that you can do in terms
of having real impact.

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:34.740
The other thing that's happening

00:23:34.740 --> 00:23:38.400
is the emergence of no code tools.

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:40.170
There's no code software

00:23:40.170 --> 00:23:41.970
and this is all about democratizing

00:23:41.970 --> 00:23:45.030
and making software development accessible

00:23:45.030 --> 00:23:46.020
to a lot more people.

00:23:46.020 --> 00:23:48.270
So there's no code software tools

00:23:48.270 --> 00:23:50.790
and then there's no code
machine learning tools.

00:23:50.790 --> 00:23:51.780
And you and I mentioned,

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:55.530
before that generative AI
is going to democratize

00:23:55.530 --> 00:23:56.610
software development,

00:23:56.610 --> 00:24:00.060
like English will be the
new programming language.

00:24:00.060 --> 00:24:02.970
These no code tools are also about,

00:24:02.970 --> 00:24:06.183
how do I do point and
click to get my job done?

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:09.173
And this is yet another the trend.

00:24:09.173 --> 00:24:13.560
When you think about product development,

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:16.980
having these I-aesthetics, A-aesthetics,

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:19.290
how do you make something
really simple to use?

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:21.720
How do you make something
really easy to use,

00:24:21.720 --> 00:24:24.180
is not just about science education,

00:24:24.180 --> 00:24:28.260
it also needs a lot of broad liberal arts

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:29.160
kind of an education,

00:24:29.160 --> 00:24:31.410
so you really understand from a product.

00:24:31.410 --> 00:24:32.880
You can think about a product,

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:35.850
not just from the
engineering point of view,

00:24:35.850 --> 00:24:38.370
but also from the usability point of view.

00:24:38.370 --> 00:24:40.320
How will users interact with it?

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:41.940
And I think that is one of the things,

00:24:41.940 --> 00:24:44.340
again, that a university like Yale,

00:24:44.340 --> 00:24:47.400
with its rich liberal arts tradition,

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:48.660
can do a really good job of.

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:51.450
How do you train engineers,

00:24:51.450 --> 00:24:54.510
who not only know how to build a product,

00:24:54.510 --> 00:24:56.130
but who can actually build a product

00:24:56.130 --> 00:24:57.930
that's delightful to use

00:24:57.930 --> 00:25:00.870
and delightful to use for
a lot of other people.

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:05.520
When you're talking about, how
do I develop a software tool

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:07.170
that someone who doesn't know anything

00:25:07.170 --> 00:25:08.847
about software uses it.

00:25:08.847 --> 00:25:10.140
And there's a lot of work

00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:12.453
that has to go into designing that stuff.

00:25:13.680 --> 00:25:17.073
So this is going to be another
trend that is important.

00:25:18.330 --> 00:25:21.665
The reason I wanted to
talk about Amazon Monitron

00:25:21.665 --> 00:25:26.580
is to show how there can be synergies

00:25:26.580 --> 00:25:28.560
with what is already available

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:31.350
and how Yale can play into that.

00:25:31.350 --> 00:25:34.290
Because there are some
strengths we bring at AWS,

00:25:34.290 --> 00:25:35.490
but there are some strengths

00:25:35.490 --> 00:25:39.270
where we are just not in the
domain, that Yale can bring.

00:25:39.270 --> 00:25:44.250
So what Monitron does, is
it uses machine learning

00:25:44.250 --> 00:25:46.920
for doing predictive
maintenance of your machines.

00:25:46.920 --> 00:25:51.030
Like it predicts when
your machine may go down

00:25:51.030 --> 00:25:54.330
and the way it does that
is we have these sensors

00:25:54.330 --> 00:25:59.010
and these sensors are
monitoring your equipment

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:02.520
for temperature and vibrations.

00:26:02.520 --> 00:26:04.290
And so, if you think of a machine,

00:26:04.290 --> 00:26:05.790
when the machine is about to break,

00:26:05.790 --> 00:26:07.410
it kind of sounds a little weird

00:26:07.410 --> 00:26:08.820
or it vibrates a little weird,

00:26:08.820 --> 00:26:11.520
it can get heated up
and so on and so forth.

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:15.840
So these sensors are put on
devices, put on machines,

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:20.082
and then they monitor the
temperature and vibrations

00:26:20.082 --> 00:26:22.710
of these devices.

00:26:22.710 --> 00:26:27.090
And then what we use is,

00:26:27.090 --> 00:26:29.763
you'll see these in these kinds of things.

00:26:31.185 --> 00:26:35.820
These sensors are installed
in these kinds of machines

00:26:35.820 --> 00:26:38.730
and then they're
streaming it to the cloud.

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:42.090
And in the cloud we detect anomalies

00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:43.680
and based on those anomalies,

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:47.220
we predict when something
is going to break.

00:26:47.220 --> 00:26:50.640
And so there are a lot of
companies, this is Baxter,

00:26:50.640 --> 00:26:53.280
but, Baxter, SKF,

00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:56.400
and a bunch of other companies, Coke,

00:26:56.400 --> 00:26:58.590
that are actually using these

00:26:58.590 --> 00:27:02.640
for predicting when their
equipment will fail.

00:27:02.640 --> 00:27:03.870
Now here's something interesting,

00:27:03.870 --> 00:27:05.640
I was actually talking about this,

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:07.380
as I mentioned, I was
talking to some people

00:27:07.380 --> 00:27:10.440
from the Yale School
of Medicine yesterday,

00:27:10.440 --> 00:27:14.190
and they said, "Well, do
you have anomaly detection

00:27:14.190 --> 00:27:18.420
that we could use to predict strokes?"

00:27:18.420 --> 00:27:22.080
Now you can imagine this,

00:27:22.080 --> 00:27:26.340
these sensors are actually
sending data to the cloud.

00:27:26.340 --> 00:27:28.380
That is a time series data,

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:30.210
where we are predicting anomalies.

00:27:30.210 --> 00:27:33.600
Now you can use those
predicting anomalies in our case

00:27:33.600 --> 00:27:36.150
to detect when a machine
is going to break,

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:37.830
but you can imagine you can do this

00:27:37.830 --> 00:27:40.140
for other kinds of time
series data as well,

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:42.210
one of which could be health data.

00:27:42.210 --> 00:27:47.210
Now, where could AWS and
Yale play really well

00:27:47.430 --> 00:27:49.020
with each other?

00:27:49.020 --> 00:27:51.720
If you have to do this work

00:27:51.720 --> 00:27:52.950
and if you have to do this research,

00:27:52.950 --> 00:27:56.460
you need an infrastructure
that will be able to take data

00:27:56.460 --> 00:27:58.920
from millions and billions of people,

00:27:58.920 --> 00:28:02.758
be scalable, be robust and efficient

00:28:02.758 --> 00:28:06.330
and do all of that anomaly detection.

00:28:06.330 --> 00:28:08.103
We have that infrastructure here.

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:11.190
What someone else could do is say, "Look,

00:28:11.190 --> 00:28:12.780
here is the domain expertise.

00:28:12.780 --> 00:28:15.780
Here is what the data looks
like in a normal person.

00:28:15.780 --> 00:28:17.580
Here is where the anomalies come in

00:28:17.580 --> 00:28:19.530
when someone is going to fall sick."

00:28:19.530 --> 00:28:22.740
So you could then marry
that domain expertise

00:28:22.740 --> 00:28:26.100
with our infrastructure and
then together we could build

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:27.903
some really interesting things.

00:28:28.770 --> 00:28:30.630
And by the way, I was talking to people,

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:32.040
and this is probably applicable

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:33.870
even to the school of engineering,

00:28:33.870 --> 00:28:37.470
is we have similar things
for image processing.

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:41.160
So you can imagine that you
could have radiology images

00:28:41.160 --> 00:28:44.550
being uploaded and detect anomalies.

00:28:44.550 --> 00:28:46.410
Now there's two aspects to that.

00:28:46.410 --> 00:28:48.900
You have to build
infrastructure, which we have,

00:28:48.900 --> 00:28:51.630
and then someone has to do
the research that sits on top

00:28:51.630 --> 00:28:55.083
and then together you end up
with some remarkable systems.

00:29:00.660 --> 00:29:03.930
The other thing that
is coming across a lot

00:29:03.930 --> 00:29:06.300
is sustainable mobility.

00:29:06.300 --> 00:29:08.220
So if you look at the automotive industry,

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:10.680
that's really thinking
about electrification,

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:14.760
about getting greener, using batteries.

00:29:14.760 --> 00:29:17.280
And so one of the things
that's happening now a lot

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:18.940
is using geospatial data

00:29:19.980 --> 00:29:23.010
to figure out driving habits
and optimizing things.

00:29:23.010 --> 00:29:26.190
So BMW has been working with us

00:29:26.190 --> 00:29:30.450
and they do things like
predicting the impact of traffic,

00:29:30.450 --> 00:29:32.580
where do you put charging stations?

00:29:32.580 --> 00:29:33.810
And a lot of other stuff.

00:29:33.810 --> 00:29:36.150
So all kinds of data analysis going on,

00:29:36.150 --> 00:29:38.700
but this is yet another
modality to the data,

00:29:38.700 --> 00:29:41.550
which is geospatial and how do I use it

00:29:41.550 --> 00:29:43.413
for predicting user behavior.

00:29:48.570 --> 00:29:50.670
And so, they're basically looking at this

00:29:50.670 --> 00:29:53.670
for predicting affinity
for electric vehicles,

00:29:53.670 --> 00:29:55.890
depending on driving tendencies

00:29:55.890 --> 00:29:57.663
and where people do their drivings.

00:30:03.614 --> 00:30:08.614
Okay. I talked about
all of the applications,

00:30:10.680 --> 00:30:13.740
first about the rapid rate
at which science is going,

00:30:13.740 --> 00:30:15.480
then about all of the applications,

00:30:15.480 --> 00:30:19.260
then where there are still
research problems in doing that.

00:30:19.260 --> 00:30:22.890
And then the fourth, this
one is yet another place

00:30:22.890 --> 00:30:25.710
where there's a lot of
research to be done,

00:30:25.710 --> 00:30:28.650
but a lot of interdisciplinary
research to be done.

00:30:28.650 --> 00:30:31.620
Which again, knowing the heritage of Yale,

00:30:31.620 --> 00:30:34.110
is a great place for Yale to be in,

00:30:34.110 --> 00:30:39.110
because the spending on AI is
going to just go astronomical.

00:30:41.280 --> 00:30:45.330
I mean, you can look at
all kinds of estimates.

00:30:45.330 --> 00:30:47.340
Those estimates are always
hundreds of billions

00:30:47.340 --> 00:30:48.173
or trillions.

00:30:48.173 --> 00:30:50.340
So it's going to be a
pretty large industry.

00:30:50.340 --> 00:30:54.240
But there's also a need to be
able to use it responsibly,

00:30:54.240 --> 00:30:57.630
to be able to put in the right
amount of guardrails in it.

00:30:57.630 --> 00:31:01.260
And that is what is not just
a computer science problem,

00:31:01.260 --> 00:31:04.758
that's a computer science
with public policy,

00:31:04.758 --> 00:31:08.280
there are legal implications of it.

00:31:08.280 --> 00:31:10.320
And so that is a holistic thing

00:31:10.320 --> 00:31:11.610
and I think that is where,

00:31:11.610 --> 00:31:15.150
again, universities can play a big role,

00:31:15.150 --> 00:31:18.450
especially in bringing
interdisciplinary work.

00:31:18.450 --> 00:31:22.866
There's just a ton of research
that can be done here,

00:31:22.866 --> 00:31:27.866
in terms of this is the core
computer science part of it,

00:31:28.500 --> 00:31:32.700
in terms of fairness, bias
detection, toxicity and so on.

00:31:32.700 --> 00:31:34.680
In terms of explainability,

00:31:34.680 --> 00:31:37.920
why is the model behaving
the way it is behaving?

00:31:37.920 --> 00:31:42.000
In terms of robustness,
how do you make it safer

00:31:42.000 --> 00:31:43.620
from all kinds of injection attacks

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:45.150
and so on and so forth?

00:31:45.150 --> 00:31:48.300
In terms of privacy, governance and so on.

00:31:48.300 --> 00:31:50.460
So especially with generative AI,

00:31:50.460 --> 00:31:52.260
especially with large language models,

00:31:52.260 --> 00:31:54.000
there's a lot of work to be done in this,

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:59.000
but there's also a lot
of work, so we do this,

00:31:59.070 --> 00:32:00.480
and if you want to get a sense

00:32:00.480 --> 00:32:03.720
of what is useful to industry,

00:32:03.720 --> 00:32:05.730
take a look at some of
these service cards,

00:32:05.730 --> 00:32:10.530
because they talk about how
customers approach this problem

00:32:10.530 --> 00:32:13.170
and how customers would
like to get insights

00:32:13.170 --> 00:32:14.373
into these problems.

00:32:15.450 --> 00:32:17.040
But it's just not there,

00:32:17.040 --> 00:32:22.040
because there's a lot of work
on regulatory agencies now.

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:25.680
There's a regulation called ISO 40:2001

00:32:25.680 --> 00:32:28.170
that is making its way through,

00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:33.170
in terms of how you regulate
or how you work on AI and ML.

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:35.610
And that's all a great place

00:32:35.610 --> 00:32:38.310
for a lot of interdisciplinary
work to be done,

00:32:38.310 --> 00:32:39.780
that's going to have a huge impact

00:32:39.780 --> 00:32:43.470
on how we deal with AI and ML.

00:32:43.470 --> 00:32:46.560
Finally, I want to talk
about a couple of things

00:32:46.560 --> 00:32:48.450
that we do in my team.

00:32:48.450 --> 00:32:50.920
We also run the Amazon Research Awards

00:32:51.840 --> 00:32:56.840
and we have awarded over $35
million in support of research.

00:32:57.540 --> 00:33:01.113
And in the last, what, eight years,

00:33:02.040 --> 00:33:05.070
we have funded more than 220 universities

00:33:05.070 --> 00:33:06.540
in about 40 countries.

00:33:06.540 --> 00:33:08.700
So it's a pretty global spread,

00:33:08.700 --> 00:33:12.090
is actually funding
that we also do to Yale.

00:33:12.090 --> 00:33:15.990
So I know that at this point

00:33:15.990 --> 00:33:20.990
we have actually two grants going on.

00:33:21.720 --> 00:33:26.370
One, with I think in
the genomics department

00:33:26.370 --> 00:33:27.300
and the lower one

00:33:27.300 --> 00:33:29.650
is probably in the computer
science department.

00:33:31.140 --> 00:33:33.570
But this happens every year.

00:33:33.570 --> 00:33:37.053
So if people have good
ideas, submit to these.

00:33:38.790 --> 00:33:42.900
And in addition, because this
is getting so interesting,

00:33:42.900 --> 00:33:45.573
we're also investing a
hundred million dollars

00:33:45.573 --> 00:33:49.980
in helping customers take
generative AI projects

00:33:49.980 --> 00:33:52.590
from concepts to production.

00:33:52.590 --> 00:33:57.210
And so, these are all really
good areas to be focusing on,

00:33:57.210 --> 00:33:58.530
in terms of your projects,

00:33:58.530 --> 00:34:01.743
in terms of your research,
in terms of enabling things.

00:34:04.500 --> 00:34:08.490
So that's all I have and
I'm happy to take questions,

00:34:08.490 --> 00:34:12.330
but really the key
areas I would talk about

00:34:12.330 --> 00:34:14.940
is foundation models

00:34:14.940 --> 00:34:18.180
and there'll be a multiplicity of models,

00:34:18.180 --> 00:34:20.130
there's some research to be done there.

00:34:22.110 --> 00:34:25.800
The infrastructure, so we
can actually reduce the cost

00:34:25.800 --> 00:34:29.580
by orders of magnitude, make
it much more accessible,

00:34:29.580 --> 00:34:32.730
the kind of applications,
I talked about healthcare,

00:34:32.730 --> 00:34:34.470
I talked about software development,

00:34:34.470 --> 00:34:38.970
but, contact centers,
industrial manufacturing,

00:34:38.970 --> 00:34:41.730
document processing, all of these

00:34:41.730 --> 00:34:44.460
are going to get innovations.

00:34:44.460 --> 00:34:46.563
And then finally, education and training.

00:34:47.400 --> 00:34:51.000
There's actually a really
interesting work that showed,

00:34:51.000 --> 00:34:53.100
this was done by BCG.

00:34:53.100 --> 00:34:54.630
So we did work with Accenture

00:34:54.630 --> 00:34:56.730
that showed if you use generative AI,

00:34:56.730 --> 00:34:59.700
you become up to 50% more productive.

00:34:59.700 --> 00:35:01.410
BCG did similar work,

00:35:01.410 --> 00:35:05.040
they actually got similar
results in terms of productivity.

00:35:05.040 --> 00:35:07.920
But one of the interesting insights

00:35:07.920 --> 00:35:10.230
that came out of their research

00:35:10.230 --> 00:35:12.300
was that for beginners,

00:35:12.300 --> 00:35:15.459
the jump in productivity was much higher.

00:35:15.459 --> 00:35:18.870
And so think of it as if you
are a new software engineer

00:35:18.870 --> 00:35:21.300
going to a company, you take a long time.

00:35:21.300 --> 00:35:23.760
In fact, I was talking
to Dean Brock about this,

00:35:23.760 --> 00:35:26.790
like, we have this Amazon
universities and all that,

00:35:26.790 --> 00:35:29.490
which is we are trying to get people up,

00:35:29.490 --> 00:35:32.130
familiar with the infrastructure we have

00:35:32.130 --> 00:35:34.470
and that's a long ramp up process.

00:35:34.470 --> 00:35:37.200
Imagine if things are getting automated,

00:35:37.200 --> 00:35:38.820
that becomes much easier.

00:35:38.820 --> 00:35:41.730
So it's actually a great leveler,

00:35:41.730 --> 00:35:45.690
in terms of how you up-skill employees.

00:35:45.690 --> 00:35:48.690
Now, if generative AI is a great new tool

00:35:48.690 --> 00:35:50.733
for up-skilling employees,

00:35:51.840 --> 00:35:56.130
ultimately a university's
job is to up-skill society.

00:35:56.130 --> 00:35:58.500
And so I think it's really
important for universities,

00:35:58.500 --> 00:36:01.950
especially eminent universities like Yale,

00:36:01.950 --> 00:36:06.950
to think about what would
up-skilling society,

00:36:08.040 --> 00:36:12.183
up-skilling students 10 to
15 years from now look like?

00:36:13.620 --> 00:36:15.210
And the question, and the thought

00:36:15.210 --> 00:36:17.340
that I would leave you with,

00:36:17.340 --> 00:36:21.270
is if Yale were founded
today, how would it be run?

00:36:21.270 --> 00:36:23.940
How would all of these
technology be integrated

00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:25.743
into how you operate?

00:36:27.030 --> 00:36:28.383
Okay, that's all I have.

00:36:29.328 --> 00:36:32.328
(audience clapping)

00:36:36.430 --> 00:36:39.016
- Okay, thanks. Thanks so much, Bratin.

00:36:39.016 --> 00:36:42.183
(audience murmuring)

00:36:45.870 --> 00:36:50.870
So I think a question to this last point

00:36:50.940 --> 00:36:52.380
that I would love to know,

00:36:52.380 --> 00:36:57.380
is, so let's say we were
designing this new version of Yale

00:36:57.780 --> 00:36:59.400
and one of the first
things we wanted to do

00:36:59.400 --> 00:37:02.213
was to teach kind of a flagship course

00:37:02.213 --> 00:37:07.213
called generative AI for, I
don't know, for undergraduates,

00:37:07.560 --> 00:37:09.330
for non-majors or something like that.

00:37:09.330 --> 00:37:13.530
What would be the
elements you would include

00:37:13.530 --> 00:37:16.170
and how would you bring this
kind of larger population

00:37:16.170 --> 00:37:18.420
into this sphere of, what I think,

00:37:18.420 --> 00:37:19.770
people are a little worried about,

00:37:19.770 --> 00:37:23.640
may be kind of seen as
exclusive in some way?

00:37:23.640 --> 00:37:25.383
How do we bring everybody in?

00:37:26.580 --> 00:37:29.040
- So I would look at it from two aspects.

00:37:29.040 --> 00:37:34.040
One is a lot of content
creation will be assisted

00:37:35.100 --> 00:37:36.123
by generative AI.

00:37:37.230 --> 00:37:40.780
And so, let's say I am an artist

00:37:43.020 --> 00:37:46.710
or I am someone that is writing drafts.

00:37:46.710 --> 00:37:49.680
The first draft, a few years from now,

00:37:49.680 --> 00:37:53.730
will probably be created by
a generative AI assistant,

00:37:53.730 --> 00:37:55.020
that then you improve.

00:37:55.020 --> 00:37:57.390
So the generative AI doesn't take away

00:37:57.390 --> 00:37:58.500
the role of the human,

00:37:58.500 --> 00:38:00.720
generative AI, at least 'till now,

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:04.410
doesn't have the creativity
that a human has.

00:38:04.410 --> 00:38:05.730
But what it can get to,

00:38:05.730 --> 00:38:08.670
is it can actually accelerate your work.

00:38:08.670 --> 00:38:09.840
And so previously,

00:38:09.840 --> 00:38:11.580
let's say you would have to create a draft

00:38:11.580 --> 00:38:13.800
and you're thinking a long
time and you're writing it

00:38:13.800 --> 00:38:15.630
and you're spending weeks over it,

00:38:15.630 --> 00:38:18.303
it can probably accelerate
it to a couple of hours.

00:38:21.641 --> 00:38:26.641
And that applies as much to the
non-engineering parts of it,

00:38:26.799 --> 00:38:30.060
there's a lot of creative
output happening there.

00:38:30.060 --> 00:38:32.730
And so the things that I would look at,

00:38:32.730 --> 00:38:36.273
is how do you use it as an
assistant in daily work?

00:38:37.350 --> 00:38:42.300
How do you try to maybe automate
some of the tedious work

00:38:42.300 --> 00:38:43.560
that you do?

00:38:43.560 --> 00:38:48.390
And how do you make sure that
people outside of engineering,

00:38:48.390 --> 00:38:52.530
outside of computer science,
are aware of these tools

00:38:52.530 --> 00:38:54.870
and are thinking about
how to integrate that

00:38:54.870 --> 00:38:56.020
into their daily lives?

00:38:57.780 --> 00:38:59.190
The way I look at it almost,

00:38:59.190 --> 00:39:03.450
is this is like the
emergence of the internet

00:39:03.450 --> 00:39:04.440
and today,

00:39:04.440 --> 00:39:08.160
everybody outside of CS
and engineering uses email.

00:39:08.160 --> 00:39:11.996
Like, email has become a
natural way of doing work.

00:39:11.996 --> 00:39:14.540
Messaging and all this
has become a natural way

00:39:14.540 --> 00:39:15.753
of doing work.

00:39:16.890 --> 00:39:19.710
And so, how do you
integrate these technologies

00:39:19.710 --> 00:39:21.840
into your way of working?

00:39:21.840 --> 00:39:24.000
The difference with previous technologies,

00:39:24.000 --> 00:39:28.080
the difference with
prior technical changes

00:39:28.080 --> 00:39:30.060
is that prior technical changes

00:39:30.060 --> 00:39:33.690
were not a creative assistant, right?

00:39:33.690 --> 00:39:36.030
This is the thing that can get integrated

00:39:36.030 --> 00:39:37.500
into the creative workflow

00:39:37.500 --> 00:39:42.500
and that is where I think we
would also look to universities

00:39:42.540 --> 00:39:45.513
to help us understand how
these should get integrated.

00:39:47.100 --> 00:39:51.030
- So, like the emergence
of the calculator,

00:39:51.030 --> 00:39:52.920
there was a lot of anxiety

00:39:52.920 --> 00:39:54.570
about how this is going to change

00:39:54.570 --> 00:39:56.580
the way we think about education.

00:39:56.580 --> 00:39:59.190
The calculator has the virtue,

00:39:59.190 --> 00:40:02.940
that it predictably gives
you the correct answer.

00:40:02.940 --> 00:40:07.110
So there's a funny
phenomenon you mentioned,

00:40:07.110 --> 00:40:09.423
which is AI hallucination,

00:40:11.310 --> 00:40:14.220
and I think those of us
in science and engineering

00:40:14.220 --> 00:40:17.370
are kind of particularly unnerved by this.

00:40:17.370 --> 00:40:19.950
So you ask generative AI a question

00:40:19.950 --> 00:40:21.390
that has to do with a research topic

00:40:21.390 --> 00:40:24.026
and sometimes it just
gives you the wrong answer.

00:40:24.026 --> 00:40:25.420
(Jeffrey laughing)

00:40:25.420 --> 00:40:27.057
- And it gives sometimes
gives you that answer

00:40:27.057 --> 00:40:28.320
in a very expert-like way.

00:40:28.320 --> 00:40:30.470
- Exactly. It sounds
like the right answer.

00:40:32.130 --> 00:40:34.020
What's your sort of response to that,

00:40:34.020 --> 00:40:35.760
in terms of what you're
seeing in the research front?

00:40:35.760 --> 00:40:39.240
Can we ameliorate that, can
we think about it in a way

00:40:39.240 --> 00:40:40.830
that makes it sort of seem

00:40:40.830 --> 00:40:43.140
like more of a bonafide research tool

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:44.670
for serious engineering?

00:40:44.670 --> 00:40:47.533
- Yeah, so I think there's
a lot of research going on

00:40:47.533 --> 00:40:50.493
in how you deal with that
and how you mitigate that.

00:40:51.330 --> 00:40:52.950
There are techniques like RAG,

00:40:52.950 --> 00:40:55.295
retrieval augmented generation,

00:40:55.295 --> 00:40:57.810
that reduce it to a large extent.

00:40:57.810 --> 00:41:00.120
Some of the grounding
things that say, "Okay,

00:41:00.120 --> 00:41:02.670
I generated it, but I got it from here."

00:41:02.670 --> 00:41:04.775
So it's kind of an accelerator.

00:41:04.775 --> 00:41:07.590
You look at the output, you
don't completely rely on it,

00:41:07.590 --> 00:41:09.040
but you can quickly check it.

00:41:11.370 --> 00:41:13.380
And I think there's a lot
of research to be done.

00:41:13.380 --> 00:41:18.380
This I think is a really good
area for fundamental research

00:41:19.980 --> 00:41:22.320
and especially I think
even to a large extent

00:41:22.320 --> 00:41:24.930
these neural nets are
still not fully understood,

00:41:24.930 --> 00:41:25.860
how these are operating.

00:41:25.860 --> 00:41:30.600
And I think that's a great
area for university research.

00:41:30.600 --> 00:41:33.540
- So in terms of where,

00:41:33.540 --> 00:41:36.360
you mentioned, sort of,
great institutions like Yale,

00:41:36.360 --> 00:41:37.830
of course we're all big fans of Yale,

00:41:37.830 --> 00:41:41.460
but where do you see our
comparative advantage

00:41:41.460 --> 00:41:44.100
in this space, in terms
of preparing students

00:41:44.100 --> 00:41:46.440
to enter this kind of new,

00:41:46.440 --> 00:41:49.860
I mean really novel,
transformed workforce?

00:41:49.860 --> 00:41:52.830
- I think there are a couple of aspects.

00:41:52.830 --> 00:41:57.830
One is, Yale has some of the
best research and teaching

00:42:02.801 --> 00:42:07.650
and professors across a variety of fields.

00:42:07.650 --> 00:42:11.520
And a lot of this,
applying AI to healthcare,

00:42:11.520 --> 00:42:14.850
requires collaboration between people

00:42:14.850 --> 00:42:15.930
who know medicine deeply

00:42:15.930 --> 00:42:17.610
and people who know
computer science deeply

00:42:17.610 --> 00:42:19.170
and people who know engineering deeply

00:42:19.170 --> 00:42:22.350
because you are doing
these sensors and so on.

00:42:22.350 --> 00:42:25.833
There's a lot of implications
on society and public policy.

00:42:27.420 --> 00:42:30.570
One of the examples that
I would love to see,

00:42:30.570 --> 00:42:32.880
and we would love to collaborate on that,

00:42:32.880 --> 00:42:34.897
is there are customers who say,

00:42:34.897 --> 00:42:36.930
"I have all kinds of legal contracts

00:42:36.930 --> 00:42:38.940
that I sign with everyone.

00:42:38.940 --> 00:42:40.650
Can you quickly,

00:42:40.650 --> 00:42:43.530
if I upload three or four
of these legal contracts,

00:42:43.530 --> 00:42:44.640
can you quickly tell me

00:42:44.640 --> 00:42:47.490
if there are conflicting
portions in there?"

00:42:47.490 --> 00:42:51.150
And so an interaction with the law school

00:42:51.150 --> 00:42:53.280
and someone from the
computer science department

00:42:53.280 --> 00:42:54.113
would be great.

00:42:54.113 --> 00:42:55.650
Public policy is ripe for this.

00:42:55.650 --> 00:42:58.350
So I think one of the things
that Yale can really do

00:42:58.350 --> 00:43:00.990
is that interdisciplinary portion.

00:43:00.990 --> 00:43:02.340
And then the second part,

00:43:02.340 --> 00:43:06.210
which I a think university
like Yale can do,

00:43:06.210 --> 00:43:09.330
that's probably generally
true of a lot of universities,

00:43:09.330 --> 00:43:10.950
but I think more true of Yale,

00:43:10.950 --> 00:43:14.880
because Yale has this really
strong theoretical foundation

00:43:14.880 --> 00:43:17.610
to everything that it has done,

00:43:17.610 --> 00:43:19.740
is really furthering the understanding

00:43:19.740 --> 00:43:21.900
at a theoretical basic level

00:43:21.900 --> 00:43:24.900
of what is going on with
some of these neural nets.

00:43:24.900 --> 00:43:27.480
And as you were saying Dean Brock,

00:43:27.480 --> 00:43:30.510
I think there's a lot of need
to get into the system side

00:43:30.510 --> 00:43:32.190
because when you get into the system side,

00:43:32.190 --> 00:43:35.400
you're truly impacting
society and shaping products.

00:43:35.400 --> 00:43:40.400
But I think without losing
that core theoretical strength,

00:43:40.770 --> 00:43:42.900
because someone has to do that for us

00:43:42.900 --> 00:43:45.183
to really truly understand these systems.

00:43:47.280 --> 00:43:48.540
- Alright, well very good.

00:43:48.540 --> 00:43:51.600
I think I've exhausted my
short list of questions,

00:43:51.600 --> 00:43:54.150
but I know there must
be many in the audience.

00:43:54.150 --> 00:43:56.250
Is there anyone who?

00:43:56.250 --> 00:43:58.750
Okay, you're first hand
up, in the front row here.

00:43:59.760 --> 00:44:00.593
Oh, here's comes mic.

00:44:00.593 --> 00:44:03.420
- Oh, perfect. Hi, My
name's Neil Sagitava,

00:44:03.420 --> 00:44:06.150
I'm an undergrad,
studying computer science.

00:44:06.150 --> 00:44:10.080
I actually interned at AWS this
past summer in Santa Clara.

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:12.002
So good to meet you as as my boss.

00:44:12.002 --> 00:44:13.920
(all laughing)

00:44:13.920 --> 00:44:16.680
My question, which I got
asked by family and friends

00:44:16.680 --> 00:44:17.760
for the past few months,

00:44:17.760 --> 00:44:21.270
was where is AWS in the conversation of,

00:44:21.270 --> 00:44:23.310
where's AWS's chat GPT?

00:44:23.310 --> 00:44:24.780
So maybe you could discuss,

00:44:24.780 --> 00:44:27.660
and you mentioned in your roadmap earlier,

00:44:27.660 --> 00:44:29.460
just highlight some of AWS's strengths

00:44:29.460 --> 00:44:31.080
in that, moving forward.

00:44:31.080 --> 00:44:36.080
- I think our key strength
is making systems fit

00:44:37.260 --> 00:44:40.137
for enterprise usage.

00:44:40.137 --> 00:44:43.470
Now, I won't say it's easy,

00:44:43.470 --> 00:44:46.440
but it's one thing to get something out

00:44:46.440 --> 00:44:48.840
that a lot of consumers can use,

00:44:48.840 --> 00:44:51.510
but when you're thinking of an
about an enterprise setting,

00:44:51.510 --> 00:44:54.990
you really have a very different
bar in terms of robustness,

00:44:54.990 --> 00:44:57.660
in terms of accuracy
and so on and so forth.

00:44:57.660 --> 00:44:59.670
And so now you're seeing,
I'll give you an example,

00:44:59.670 --> 00:45:02.580
think of CodeWhisperer,
that I talked about.

00:45:02.580 --> 00:45:04.710
You can, and other companies have,

00:45:04.710 --> 00:45:05.783
come out with tools of,

00:45:05.783 --> 00:45:09.840
here is how you use
generative AI for software.

00:45:09.840 --> 00:45:11.490
We came out with CodeWhisperer

00:45:11.490 --> 00:45:14.550
and a lot of our time was spent

00:45:14.550 --> 00:45:17.580
in putting accurate license attribution.

00:45:17.580 --> 00:45:20.190
So what you do, is when
you're generating code,

00:45:20.190 --> 00:45:23.940
you have to make sure
it is not a copy paste

00:45:23.940 --> 00:45:25.770
of somebody else's code.

00:45:25.770 --> 00:45:28.680
And if that's somebody else's
code has a proper license,

00:45:28.680 --> 00:45:30.420
you've got to be able to generate code

00:45:30.420 --> 00:45:33.000
that has that copyright attribute.

00:45:33.000 --> 00:45:34.883
We were the first to come out with,

00:45:34.883 --> 00:45:36.750
here is software development

00:45:36.750 --> 00:45:39.030
and here is the
appropriate license for it.

00:45:39.030 --> 00:45:40.470
And if you don't have that,

00:45:40.470 --> 00:45:42.660
you can use that for research projects

00:45:42.660 --> 00:45:46.440
or just fun hackathons, you
cannot use that in industry.

00:45:46.440 --> 00:45:48.900
So our core thing is how do you make

00:45:48.900 --> 00:45:51.930
these appropriate and
suitable for industry?

00:45:51.930 --> 00:45:53.370
And that I think is a core strength.

00:45:53.370 --> 00:45:54.870
That is the place we innovate.

00:45:56.550 --> 00:45:59.340
- That's great. That applies
to scientific research as well,

00:45:59.340 --> 00:46:02.370
where you need appropriate
citations across the space.

00:46:02.370 --> 00:46:03.420
Sorry, next question.

00:46:05.190 --> 00:46:07.340
- Thank you for an amazing talk.

00:46:10.320 --> 00:46:13.203
Yes, thank you Bratin for an amazing talk.

00:46:14.400 --> 00:46:17.523
I'm Sam Hayek, I'm a fellow
in one of the Yale colleges.

00:46:19.410 --> 00:46:22.143
Five, ten years from now,

00:46:24.480 --> 00:46:28.780
Amazon will need to
differentiate its AI products

00:46:29.850 --> 00:46:33.030
to compete with, let's say the Microsofts

00:46:33.030 --> 00:46:35.350
and the other competitors

00:46:36.360 --> 00:46:40.747
and one way obviously, is to
make the products user friendly

00:46:42.090 --> 00:46:43.800
and so on and so forth.

00:46:43.800 --> 00:46:45.539
I'm concerned about,

00:46:45.539 --> 00:46:49.863
not for Amazon itself but all the players,

00:46:50.910 --> 00:46:55.293
about the integrity of
the fundamental data.

00:46:56.880 --> 00:47:01.600
Could you give us examples
of how you ensure integrity?

00:47:06.840 --> 00:47:10.830
Yeah, very simply make sure
that, let's say the image,

00:47:10.830 --> 00:47:14.730
one of the images that you're
feeding into the ML system

00:47:14.730 --> 00:47:19.730
is not corrupt and is not
giving the wrong results.

00:47:20.850 --> 00:47:22.470
- Yeah, I'm glad you asked that question,

00:47:22.470 --> 00:47:25.350
because that's something
that's top of mind for us.

00:47:25.350 --> 00:47:27.520
And integrity, security, privacy,

00:47:27.520 --> 00:47:29.940
this is just foundational
to everything we do,

00:47:29.940 --> 00:47:31.350
not just AI and ML

00:47:31.350 --> 00:47:34.800
and we have whole teams
that are focused on that.

00:47:34.800 --> 00:47:37.290
So we have a lot of mechanisms for that,

00:47:37.290 --> 00:47:40.110
one of the things that pretty
much every service does is,

00:47:40.110 --> 00:47:42.120
and we do in the AI space,

00:47:42.120 --> 00:47:45.150
is end-to-end encryption of everything.

00:47:45.150 --> 00:47:47.700
So you have encryption addressed,

00:47:47.700 --> 00:47:50.940
encryption in transit with
customer provided keys.

00:47:50.940 --> 00:47:55.940
So there's just no way anyone
can kind of access that data.

00:47:58.380 --> 00:48:00.456
There are a bunch of
other mechanisms we use

00:48:00.456 --> 00:48:04.530
in terms of ensuring privacy of the data

00:48:04.530 --> 00:48:09.030
and we will also be coming
up with some innovations now

00:48:09.030 --> 00:48:13.440
that actually reuse some
of the latest signs,

00:48:13.440 --> 00:48:16.260
in terms of how you do that.

00:48:16.260 --> 00:48:18.750
So this is pretty fundamental

00:48:18.750 --> 00:48:20.550
to everything we are building.

00:48:20.550 --> 00:48:22.440
One of the other things I'll also mention

00:48:22.440 --> 00:48:24.840
that a lot of customers are
doing with generative AI

00:48:24.840 --> 00:48:26.880
is they're saying, "Look, I have,"

00:48:26.880 --> 00:48:29.638
I mean it could be Yale,
it could be a company.

00:48:29.638 --> 00:48:30.960
I'll give you example.

00:48:30.960 --> 00:48:34.500
So for example, we have a
pharma company, let's say,

00:48:34.500 --> 00:48:37.560
that wants to use this generative AI

00:48:37.560 --> 00:48:39.570
and enable its chemists.

00:48:39.570 --> 00:48:41.670
And the way it wants
to enable its chemists,

00:48:41.670 --> 00:48:44.220
is they want to be able to go in

00:48:44.220 --> 00:48:47.340
and say, "Hey, give me
all of the experiments

00:48:47.340 --> 00:48:50.340
that this company has
run in the last 10 years

00:48:50.340 --> 00:48:53.040
about the side effects of this drug."

00:48:53.040 --> 00:48:55.770
And today you have to
run a lot of searches

00:48:55.770 --> 00:48:58.860
and now with you can
imagine with generative AI,

00:48:58.860 --> 00:49:00.000
it can just find it out.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:04.683
But the issue is that
that data is not public.

00:49:05.700 --> 00:49:07.830
Only that company has it.

00:49:07.830 --> 00:49:10.800
And so you need to be
able to customize models

00:49:10.800 --> 00:49:12.840
with your own data.

00:49:12.840 --> 00:49:14.190
Now, the way we do it,

00:49:14.190 --> 00:49:16.320
kind of answers the previous question,

00:49:16.320 --> 00:49:17.153
is pretty unique.

00:49:17.153 --> 00:49:20.040
The way we do it, is when
you customize the model,

00:49:20.040 --> 00:49:21.510
want to customize the model,

00:49:21.510 --> 00:49:25.560
we actually create your own
private copy of the model

00:49:25.560 --> 00:49:29.820
and then we create it in your
own virtual private cloud.

00:49:29.820 --> 00:49:31.860
So it's in your own security domain.

00:49:31.860 --> 00:49:35.220
So we have a lot of these
mechanisms that we are using

00:49:35.220 --> 00:49:38.373
to ensure the security of the data.

00:49:40.320 --> 00:49:41.820
- [Jeffrey] Good. Yep. Thanks.

00:49:43.770 --> 00:49:44.970
- [Ben] Hi, I'm Ben,

00:49:44.970 --> 00:49:48.180
I am an international
student in Yale College.

00:49:48.180 --> 00:49:50.076
I would like to ask you,

00:49:50.076 --> 00:49:53.140
which industries are least
likely to be affected by AI

00:49:53.140 --> 00:49:54.930
in your opinion?

00:49:54.930 --> 00:49:59.440
Because there is like a
debate on which occupations

00:50:00.420 --> 00:50:02.040
which industries, which jobs,

00:50:02.040 --> 00:50:04.050
are least likely to be affected

00:50:04.050 --> 00:50:07.173
in your personal opinion
in the long term with AI.

00:50:08.636 --> 00:50:11.820
- This, I think, is a
fundamental innovation

00:50:11.820 --> 00:50:15.123
that ultimately will touch
every aspect of what we do.

00:50:17.460 --> 00:50:19.803
And the way I look at it is,

00:50:20.790 --> 00:50:22.650
it'll generate so much new stuff.

00:50:22.650 --> 00:50:26.610
Net technology has been,
at least in my view,

00:50:26.610 --> 00:50:31.610
net technology has been a
positive force for society,

00:50:33.000 --> 00:50:34.650
a positive force for humanity.

00:50:34.650 --> 00:50:37.830
We have become more
productive, we do a lot more.

00:50:37.830 --> 00:50:39.630
And I think this is yet another

00:50:39.630 --> 00:50:41.490
of those transformational technologies

00:50:41.490 --> 00:50:42.590
that will do the same.

00:50:43.613 --> 00:50:45.450
And I suspect just like the internet,

00:50:45.450 --> 00:50:48.090
just like some of the key technical parts,

00:50:48.090 --> 00:50:50.590
it's going to be integrated
into everything we do.

00:50:54.590 --> 00:50:57.363
Ultimately, we humans create.

00:50:58.860 --> 00:51:01.290
Ultimately we do some form of creation.

00:51:01.290 --> 00:51:03.603
And this I think will
be an assistant in that.

00:51:06.150 --> 00:51:06.983
- Next question.

00:51:08.370 --> 00:51:10.770
- [Andrew] Andrew, office of development,

00:51:10.770 --> 00:51:14.700
you mentioned using AI
to help with interactions

00:51:14.700 --> 00:51:16.650
between patients and doctors.

00:51:16.650 --> 00:51:19.860
I was wondering if you
foresee any unique roadblocks

00:51:19.860 --> 00:51:23.700
with medicine with
privacy concerns or HIPAA,

00:51:23.700 --> 00:51:24.600
anything like that

00:51:24.600 --> 00:51:28.260
that may make it more
difficult to be incorporated?

00:51:28.260 --> 00:51:31.953
- So the things that I mentioned
were all HIPAA compliant.

00:51:32.940 --> 00:51:37.560
I think in medicine you
obviously have a much higher bar,

00:51:37.560 --> 00:51:41.100
because you don't want it in situations

00:51:41.100 --> 00:51:43.860
where the errors could be in there.

00:51:43.860 --> 00:51:46.860
So the bar there will be higher,

00:51:46.860 --> 00:51:49.170
and probably at least initially,

00:51:49.170 --> 00:51:52.380
it'll be a lot more of an
assistant functionality,

00:51:52.380 --> 00:51:54.690
where there's always a human in the loop

00:51:54.690 --> 00:51:57.030
that is doing the checking
of the verification.

00:51:57.030 --> 00:52:00.600
So let's say instead of a
human creating the whole thing

00:52:00.600 --> 00:52:02.250
that took them an hour,

00:52:02.250 --> 00:52:05.070
it'll probably be a human
verifying the output

00:52:05.070 --> 00:52:07.890
and doing edits as needed,
that takes them 10 minutes.

00:52:07.890 --> 00:52:11.400
So it's really going to
be a productivity enhancer

00:52:11.400 --> 00:52:12.843
rather than a replacer.

00:52:16.437 --> 00:52:17.880
- [Michael] Hi, I am Michael Cohen.

00:52:17.880 --> 00:52:19.947
I'm a public citizen here

00:52:19.947 --> 00:52:23.917
and there was an old
expression in computing,

00:52:23.917 --> 00:52:25.364
"Garbage in, garbage out."

00:52:25.364 --> 00:52:27.510
If you look at the data that's out there,

00:52:27.510 --> 00:52:29.340
there's a lot of garbage out there.

00:52:29.340 --> 00:52:30.450
How do you tell the difference

00:52:30.450 --> 00:52:33.600
between like Newsmax
and the New York Times?

00:52:33.600 --> 00:52:36.933
- Yeah! So there is a huge,

00:52:37.897 --> 00:52:39.534
and I didn't go into that,

00:52:39.534 --> 00:52:43.350
there is a lot of
engineering work we put in,

00:52:43.350 --> 00:52:45.300
but there's a lot of science as well.

00:52:45.300 --> 00:52:48.000
And there's actually two parts to this.

00:52:48.000 --> 00:52:50.400
So when we train our models,

00:52:50.400 --> 00:52:53.940
we have an entire team
that is curating the data.

00:52:53.940 --> 00:52:55.950
So that's curating the data,

00:52:55.950 --> 00:52:57.840
not just for the kind of content,

00:52:57.840 --> 00:53:00.960
but also for things like
bias and toxicity and so on.

00:53:00.960 --> 00:53:04.050
So you don't want to train the
model with inappropriate data

00:53:04.050 --> 00:53:07.529
of all kinds. So that's one aspect of it.

00:53:07.529 --> 00:53:10.500
And we have huge teams
that are working on that.

00:53:10.500 --> 00:53:15.450
Like, lots of people whose job solely,

00:53:15.450 --> 00:53:18.723
is to not just collect
the data but verify it.

00:53:19.560 --> 00:53:22.950
But I also think there's a
lot of research to be done.

00:53:22.950 --> 00:53:24.990
Now there's a second aspect of that

00:53:24.990 --> 00:53:29.460
which is how do you use
the minimum amount of data

00:53:29.460 --> 00:53:31.323
to get the desired outcome.

00:53:32.400 --> 00:53:35.280
So there is a lot of work that shows

00:53:35.280 --> 00:53:39.360
that if you had a better
way of curating your data,

00:53:39.360 --> 00:53:41.640
you might be able to get the same result

00:53:41.640 --> 00:53:42.930
with fewer training cycles.

00:53:42.930 --> 00:53:44.640
So there's work to be done there

00:53:44.640 --> 00:53:46.170
and I think there's work to be done,

00:53:46.170 --> 00:53:48.540
there's a lot of research to be done

00:53:48.540 --> 00:53:52.200
in how do you more efficiently
curate these data sources.

00:53:52.200 --> 00:53:55.170
So today it's a pretty, not a pretty,

00:53:55.170 --> 00:53:57.540
it's a very labor intensive effort

00:53:57.540 --> 00:54:02.540
and that, in many situations
is when we are training

00:54:02.730 --> 00:54:04.560
these large language models.

00:54:04.560 --> 00:54:08.460
In many situations that
data collection and curation

00:54:08.460 --> 00:54:12.033
and filtering effort is
actually the larger bottleneck.

00:54:14.310 --> 00:54:15.210
- That's another good role

00:54:15.210 --> 00:54:19.500
for discerning Yale
undergraduates to play, I suppose.

